Fix SIGTRAP traps in 30 non-void T-Coffee functions that fall off the end

clang lowers the C++ undefined behaviour "control reaches end of non-void
function" to the llvm `unreachable` intrinsic, and the AArch64 backend lowers
`unreachable` to the `brk #1` instruction.  On OHOS aarch64 the process
therefore dies with SIGTRAP the first time any of these functions is called
-- for example set_nproc() in util.c, which batch_main() invokes while still
printing its parameter dump.  gcc emits an ordinary epilogue in the same
situation, which is why the x86_64 binaries shipped and built upstream work.

All 30 functions are non-void and every call site discards the result, so
returning a zero value preserves upstream behaviour exactly while removing
the trap.  Verified against the OHOS aarch64 sysroot: the `-O0` build drops
from 31 `brk #1` sites to 1 (the libc++abi `__shim_type_info` stub, which is
unreachable at run time) and emits CLUSTAL W output byte-identical to a g++
`-O0 -fsigned-char` x86_64 reference build.

